"Introducing Claude Opus 4.7 Anthropic has released the Claude Opus 4.7 model with enhanced capabilities for advanced software engineering and challenging tasks, along with cybersecurity safeguards. Significantly improved over Claude Opus 4.6 in advanced software engineering. Handles complex, long-running tasks with rigor and consistency, and validates its own outputs. Enhanced visual capabilities with the ability to view images at higher resolution. Includes safeguards to reduce cybersecurity risks, and invites security professionals through the Cyber Verification Program. Available on Claude products and API, Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud Vertex AI, and Microsoft Foundry. New ways to create personalized images in the Gemini app The Gemini app introduces new features that leverage Nano Banana and the Google Photos library to generate personalized images based on users' personal information. Uses Google Photos and personal preferences to generate customized images without the need for long, detailed prompts. When a user requests a specific scene featuring themselves and loved ones, relevant context is automatically fetched. To protect privacy, Gemini does not train models on personal photo libraries. Set to roll out to US Google AI Plus, Pro, and Ultra subscribers within a few days. Capacity Efficiency at Meta: How Unified AI Agents Optimize Performance at Hyperscale Meta is using an AI agent platform to automatically find and resolve performance issues across its infrastructure through a 'Capacity Efficiency Program,' saving hundreds of megawatts of power and freeing up engineers' time. The AI agent platform automates resolution of infrastructure performance issues, saving hundreds of megawatts of power. Encodes the domain expertise of senior efficiency engineers into reusable skills. Uses FBDetect and AI-assisted solutions to prevent performance degradation and accelerate optimization. Targets a self-sustaining efficiency engine where AI takes charge of long-term efficiency management. MixAtlas: Uncertainty-aware Data Mixture Optimization for Multimodal LLM Midtraining Apple Machine Learning Research introduces MixAtlas, an uncertainty-aware data mixture optimization framework for multimodal LLM midtraining that improves computational efficiency and performance. MixAtlas provides computationally efficient multimodal mixture optimization through systematic domain decomposition and small-scale proxy models. Decomposes training data along two interpretable axes: image concepts and task supervision. Uses small-scale proxy models and Gaussian process surrogate models to explore the mixture space at 1/100th of full-scale training cost. Demonstrates up to 3x faster convergence and consistent 2–5% performance gains across various benchmarks compared to existing approaches. Provides particularly strong performance gains on text-rich benchmarks (ChartQA +10%, TextVQA +13%). No Need for Space Gear — Capcom's 'PRAGMATA' Joins GeForce NOW on Launch Day Capcom's sci-fi action-adventure game 'PRAGMATA' joins GeForce NOW on its launch day, available via cloud streaming. Capcom's new sci-fi action-adventure game 'PRAGMATA' joins GeForce NOW on launch day. Available for instant streaming from the cloud to nearly any device via GeForce NOW, playable without high-end hardware. The game supports ray tracing lighting and NVIDIA DLSS 4 technology for enhanced graphics quality. The story follows protagonist Hugh Williams and android girl Diana as they unravel a mystery unfolding at a lunar base. Salesforce launches Headless 360 to turn its entire platform into infrastructure for AI agents Salesforce has announced 'Headless 360,' the most ambitious architectural transformation in its 27-year history, exposing all platform capabilities as APIs, MCP tools, or CLI commands so AI agents can operate without a browser. Salesforce unveiled 'Headless 360,' which exposes all platform capabilities as APIs and more so AI agents can fully operate the system. The announcement was made at the TDX developer conference, with over 100 new tools and skills available immediately. This is Salesforce's response to the question of whether traditional CRM needs a GUI in the age of AI agents. Salesforce decided to rebuild its platform for agents two and a half years ago, related to rising enterprise AI costs. Are we getting what we paid for? How to turn AI momentum into measurable value The focus of enterprise AI is shifting from what can be built to measuring the value of investment, with lack of visibility into AI spending and high GPU costs emerging as new challenges. The focus of enterprise AI is shifting from 'what can we build' to 'how do we maximize the value of AI investments.' Red Hat's Brian Grayslie pointed to AI sprawl, rising inference costs, and lack of visibility into return on investment. Many enterprises pay for tens of thousands of Copilot licenses yet find it difficult to measure the actual impact. While cost was not a primary concern over the past two years, enterprises are now scrutinizing AI investment value closely as budget cycles come around. OpenAI debuts GPT-Rosalind, a new limited access model for life sciences, and broader Codex plugin on Github OpenAI has unveiled a specialized model called 'GPT-Rosalind' to accelerate life science research and improve efficiency, with a focus on addressing the fragmented workflow problems in existing research. OpenAI's 'GPT-Rosalind' is a new specialized model developed to improve the efficiency of life science research. The model assists with tasks that traditionally take a long time, such as generating biological hypotheses, synthesizing evidence, and designing experiments. GPT-Rosalind has been fine-tuned to provide deep understanding in the fields of genomics, protein engineering, and chemistry. It has outperformed existing models on industry benchmarks such as BixBench and LABBench2. OpenAI drastically updates Codex desktop app to use all other apps on your computer, generate images, preview webpages OpenAI has massively updated the Codex desktop app, expanding its capabilities toward a 'super app' with features such as integration with other apps, image generation, and webpage preview. The Codex desktop app has been updated with the ability to launch, click, and type into other apps on Mac and Windows. A built-in web browser enables frontend development previews, and image generation via gpt-image-1.5 is integrated. With this update, Codex becomes a powerful agent allowing developers to perform more tasks across the entire computer. It has reached 3 million weekly developers, and OpenAI is evolving Codex in line with its 'super app' vision. Anthropic releases Claude Opus 4.7, narrowly retaking lead for most powerful generally available LLM Anthropic has released a new large language model, Claude Opus 4.7, surpassing OpenAI's GPT-5.4 and Google's Gemini 3.1 Pro to become one of the most powerful general-purpose LLMs available. Claude Opus 4.7 outperforms competing models on key benchmarks including agentic coding, scaled tool use, agentic computer use, and financial analysis. It recorded an Elo score of 1753 on the GDPVal-AA knowledge work evaluation, surpassing GPT-5.4 (1674) and Gemini 3.1 Pro (1314). GPT-5.4 and Gemini 3.1 Pro still hold an edge in agentic search, multilingual Q&A, and terminal-based coding. Opus 4.7 shows performance improvements especially in 'hard' science domains such as software engineering and complex document reasoning. Available on major cloud platforms including Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud Vertex AI, and Microsoft Foundry, with API pricing maintained at $5/$25 per million tokens. 5 top cloud migration software for Infrastructure as Code (IaC) Introduces the top 5 cloud migration software tools for effectively leveraging Infrastructure as Code (IaC) in cloud migration. Cloud migration includes not just workload movement but also environment reproducibility and deployment readiness through IaC. Infros is rated as the best cloud migration software for IaC deployment, with a focus on architecture design and validation. Infros addresses the problem that migration failures originate in the architecture phase, not the provisioning phase. The platform designs and validates optimized cloud architecture, providing architectural confidence before deployment. Focuses on decision quality to help evaluate migration trade-offs such as workload placement, performance, cost, and environment design. OpenAI Agents SDK improves governance with sandbox execution OpenAI has introduced sandbox execution capabilities to the Agents SDK, enabling enterprise governance teams to deploy automated workflows with controlled risk. The new sandbox execution feature allows automated workflows to be deployed safely in enterprise environments. Addresses limitations of existing frameworks (underutilization of frontier model capabilities, lack of control visibility). Provides standardized infrastructure featuring model-native harnesses and native sandbox execution. Oscar Health used this updated SDK to significantly improve reliability in clinical records workflow automation, including extraction accuracy and understanding of patient record boundaries within files. The new capabilities improve reliability in tasks requiring coordination across multiple systems. Cadence expands AI and robotic partnerships with Nvidia, Google Cloud Cadence Design Systems is expanding its partnerships with Nvidia and Google Cloud in AI and robotics, combining physics-based simulation and AI-accelerated computing for robotic systems and system-level design. Cadence has expanded its AI and robotics partnerships through collaboration with Nvidia and Google Cloud. The partnership with Nvidia focuses on integrating AI and physics-based simulation and accelerated computing into robotic systems and system-level design. Cadence integrates its multiphysics simulation tools with Nvidia's CUDA-X libraries, AI models, and Omniverse-based simulation environments. The collaboration targets modeling and deployment of semiconductors and large-scale AI infrastructure, including robotic systems. Robot training through simulation reduces the need for real-world data collection. Factory hits $1.5B valuation to build AI coding for enterprises Factory, a startup providing AI-powered coding solutions, has raised $150 million and achieved a $1.5 billion valuation. AI-powered coding is one of the most popular and profitable use cases for generative AI. Factory is a startup developing AI agents for enterprise engineering teams. Raised $150 million led by Khosla Ventures, achieving a $1.5 billion valuation. Factory's key differentiator is the ability to switch between various foundation models such as Anthropic's Claude or DeepSeek. Morgan Stanley, Ernst & Young, and Palo Alto Networks are among Factory's customers. Luma launches AI-powered production studio with faith-focused Wonder Project AI video generation startup Luma has partnered with the faith-focused Wonder Project to establish an AI-powered production studio called Innovative Dreams, with its first project being 'The Old Stories: Moses' starring Ben Kingsley. Luma partnered with Wonder Project to establish the AI-powered production studio 'Innovative Dreams.' The first project is 'The Old Stories: Moses' starring Ben Kingsley, set to be released on Amazon Prime Video. Luma Agents is a tool that handles creative work across text, images, video, and audio. AI delivers faster, cheaper, and improved results compared to traditional virtual production and performance capture. Luma claims that generative AI can solve Hollywood's high production cost problem and make filmmaking more efficient. Physical Intelligence, a hot robotics startup, says its new robot brain can figure out tasks it was never taught San Francisco-based robotics startup Physical Intelligence has published research showing that its new model, π0.7, allows robots to perform tasks they were never explicitly trained for. Physical Intelligence's new model π0.7 can instruct robots to perform tasks they were not trained for. This is a significant advance toward a general-purpose robot brain, capable of performing unfamiliar tasks when instructed in natural language. The key claim is 'compositional generalization' — the ability to combine skills learned in different contexts to solve new problems. The model breaks from the 'rote memorization' pattern of conventional robot training. The research team confirmed that the model performed air fryer-related tasks it had never seen during training. OpenAI takes aim at Anthropic with beefed-up Codex that gives it more power over your desktop OpenAI is updating its coding AI tool Codex to gain an edge over Anthropic's Claude Code. OpenAI announced several new features and updates for Codex. Codex now operates in the background of the computer and can deploy multiple agents in parallel alongside other user tasks. This allows Codex to be used for auxiliary coding tasks such as iterating on frontend changes and testing apps. This update shares similarities with features offered by Anthropic's Claude Code. Codex aims to become a competitive coding assistant and a multifaceted tool that can be integrated into various enterprise workflows. Anthropic CPO leaves Figma's board after reports he will offer a competing product Anthropic's CPO has resigned from Figma's board of directors, further fueling investor concerns that AI labs will come to dominate software businesses, amid reports that he will launch a competing product. Anthropic CPO Krieger resigned from Figma's board. There are reports that he will launch a competing design tool. The incident has deepened investor anxiety over a 'SaaSpocalypse' — the fear that large AI labs will take over software businesses. OpenAI's big Codex update is a direct shot at Claude Code OpenAI has massively updated Codex to counter Anthropic's Claude Code, adding capabilities such as using macOS apps, generating images, and remembering past experiences. OpenAI's coding and development system Codex has been enhanced with a major update. Codex can now independently use macOS apps and run multiple agents in parallel in the background. Used for purposes such as testing frontend changes and testing apps for developers. Image generation and iteration via gpt-image-1.5, plugins for GitLab, Atlassian Rovo, and Microsoft Suite, and in-app browsing have been added. A memory feature that retains past experiences has also been added, set to increase task automation and efficiency. Google's AI Mode update lets you open links without leaving the page Google has updated Chrome's AI Mode so users can view source links from search results alongside AI Mode without navigating to a new tab, and has added a tab search feature. Clicking a source link in Chrome's AI Mode now opens the website next to AI Mode instead of in a new tab. This allows users to ask additional questions about the page's content. A feature to search within a specific selected tab in AI Mode has been added. The ability to add images or files to a search has also been included. This update is currently available to US users and will soon expand globally. Gemini can now pull from Google Photos to generate personalized images Gemini's personal intelligence feature can now leverage Google Photos data to generate personalized images that reflect users' tastes and lifestyle. Gemini's personal intelligence feature integrates with Google Photos to support image generation based on the user's personal context. Users can generate personalized images through prompts such as 'design my dream home' or 'create a photo of essentials for a deserted island.' Google Photos labels are used to identify family and friends, and the Nano Banana 2 image model generates the images. Google has stated that even if users opt into the personal intelligence feature, their personal Google Photos library will not be used directly to train AI models. The feature will be rolled out first to AI Plus, Pro, and Ultra subscribers in the US, with Chrome desktop and more users to follow soon. Anthropic releases a new Opus model amid Mythos Preview buzz Anthropic has released the Claude Opus 4.7 model, which is more capable than its predecessor for software engineering tasks but has been assessed as performing below the recently announced Mythos Preview model. Anthropic has released Claude Opus 4.7, which provides stronger performance for advanced software engineering tasks. Opus 4.7 demonstrates greater 'creativity' in image analysis, instruction following, and generating slides and documents. However, Opus 4.7 scored lower than Mythos Preview on all relevant evaluations. Mythos Preview is provided privately only to select partners such as Nvidia, JPMorgan Chase, Google, Apple, and Microsoft for security reasons. Anthropic has applied additional cybersecurity protections to Opus 4.7, which will serve as a stepping stone for a broader release of Mythos-class models. Ronan Farrow on Sam Altman's 'unconstrained' relationship with the truth Ronan Farrow and Andrew Marantz have published an in-depth article in The New Yorker about OpenAI CEO Sam Altman's credibility and OpenAI's rise, illuminating Altman's business acumen and his ambiguous relationship with the truth. Ronan Farrow and Andrew Marantz published an in-depth article on OpenAI CEO Sam Altman. The article covers Altman's credibility, OpenAI's rapid rise, and his business acumen and 'ambiguous relationship with the truth.' ChatGPT's popularity has made Altman the most prominent figure in the AI industry, growing OpenAI from a nonprofit research lab into a nearly $1 trillion private company. The article includes details about the 2023 OpenAI board firing of Altman and his immediate rehiring. It also delves into Altman's personal life, investments, fundraising from the Middle East, and his reflections on his past. Building Transformer-Based NQS for Frustrated Spin Systems with NetKet Provides a tutorial on a new approach to modeling frustrated spin systems using Neural Quantum States (NQS) at the intersection of many-body physics and deep learning, and a guide to implementing Transformer-based NQS using NetKet and JAX. Neural Quantum States (NQS) are an emerging research area at the intersection of many-body physics and deep learning. Transformer's global attention mechanism is a powerful tool for capturing complex quantum correlations. This tutorial implements a Variational Monte Carlo (VMC) pipeline using NetKet and JAX to solve the frustrated J1-J2 Heisenberg spin chain. Covers building a custom Transformer-based NQS architecture and optimizing the wave function using stochastic reconfiguration (natural gradient descent). Through this guide, one can obtain a scalable and physically grounded simulation framework to explore quantum magnetism that classical exact methods cannot reach. How to Build a Universal Long-Term Memory Layer for AI Agents Using Mem0 and OpenAI A tutorial on building a universal long-term memory layer for AI agents using Mem0, OpenAI models, and ChromaDB. Building a long-term memory layer for AI agents using Mem0, OpenAI, and ChromaDB. Extracting structured memories from natural language conversations and storing them semantically. Intelligent memory retrieval and direct integration into personalized agent responses. Implementing persistent, user-scoped memory with CRUD control, semantic search, multi-user isolation, and custom configuration. A Coding Implementation to Build Multi-Agent AI Systems with SmolAgents Using Code Execution, Tool Calling, and Dynamic Orchestration A coding implementation tutorial for building multi-agent AI systems using SmolAgents with code execution, tool calling, and dynamic orchestration. Building multi-agent AI systems using the SmolAgents framework. Demonstrating how AI agents execute code, dynamically manage tools, and collaborate across multiple agents. Designing custom tools including math utilities, memory stores, and web search capabilities. Exploring CodeAgent and ToolCallingAgent paradigms and implementing multi-agent orchestration. Python Project Setup 2026: uv + Ruff + Ty + Polars Introduces the modern stack (uv, Ruff, Ty, Polars) for Python project setup, explaining how to make projects faster, cleaner, and easier to maintain. Using uv for Python installation, environment management, dependency management, locking, and command execution. Using Ruff for code linting and formatting. Using Ty for type checking. Using Polars for dataframe operations. Streamlining setup and reducing friction through tight integration of tools provided by Astral. Docker for Python & Data Projects: A Beginner's Guide A beginner's guide on using Docker to build, share, and deploy consistent environments for Python and data projects. Using Docker to solve dependency issues in Python projects and provide consistent environments. Packaging code and the entire environment (Python version, dependencies, system libraries) into an image. Creating and deploying containers that run identically from an image. Provides examples of serving machine learning models with FastAPI, multi-service pipelines with Docker Compose, and job scheduling with Cron containers. Training and Finetuning Multimodal Embedding & Reranker Models with Sentence Transformers Explains how to train and fine-tune multimodal embedding and reranker models using the Sentence Transformers library, with a case study on fine-tuning the Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B model for the Visual Document Retrieval (VDR) task. Sentence Transformers is a Python library for training embedding and reranker models. Models that process text, images, audio, and video can be trained using multimodal capabilities. Fine-tuning the Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B model for the VDR task achieved a performance improvement (NDCG@10 from 0.888 to 0.947). The fine-tuned model tomaarsen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B-vdr demonstrates performance surpassing existing VDR models. Anthropic, Claude Opus 4.7 Release - Self-Verification, 3.3x High-Resolution Vision, 13% Coding Improvement Anthropic has released its flagship model Claude Opus 4.7, which achieved outstanding results across multiple benchmarks, including self-verification, 3.3x high-resolution vision, and a 13% improvement in coding performance. Claude Opus 4.7 recorded SWE-bench Verified 87.6% and SWE-bench Pro 64.3%, surpassing GPT-5.4. Achieved high benchmark scores including GPQA Diamond 94.2%, Terminal-Bench 2.0 69.4%, and Finance Agent 64.4%. Showed 13% improved performance over Opus 4.6 on coding benchmarks. Google Gemma 4, Supports Fully Offline AI Inference on iPhone Google's open-source model Gemma 4 supports fully offline AI inference on an iPhone without an internet connection, marking an important milestone in the realization of on-device AI transition and the expansion of the edge AI ecosystem. Google Gemma 4's 31B model runs offline on an iPhone, signaling the arrival of the on-device AI era. Shows performance comparable to Qwen 3.5's 27B model, with mobile-optimized E2B and E4B variants advantageous for speed and heat management. The Google AI Edge Gallery app enables model selection and on-device inference, supporting image recognition and voice interaction without API or cloud dependency. Inference via the iPhone's GPU provides low response latency, proving that high-performance AI workloads can be handled on consumer hardware. Offline capabilities greatly expand applicability in enterprise environments such as field operations, healthcare, and data privacy-sensitive areas. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B: Agentic Coding Power, Now Open to All Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, a large language model open-sourced by Alibaba's Qwen team, uses a MoE architecture to deliver efficient agentic coding performance and surpasses existing models on multiple benchmarks. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B uses a MoE architecture with 35 billion total parameters, of which only 3 billion are used during inference, maximizing efficiency. Agentic coding performance has significantly improved over the previous version, recording SWE-bench Verified 73.4 and Terminal-Bench 2.0 51.5. In the multimodal domain, it leads Claude Sonnet 4.5 with MMMU 81.7 and RealWorldQA 85.3, with a strength in object location recognition within images. With 3B active parameters, it delivers performance comparable to 27–31B dense models, reducing GPU memory and power consumption. Compatible with the Anthropic API protocol, enabling entry into the Claude Code ecosystem, and demonstrating a case where an open-source model outperforms a paid model. ChatGPT for Excel The launch of a ChatGPT add-in for Excel provides spreadsheet creation, analysis, and update capabilities through natural language commands, improving work efficiency. Launch of a ChatGPT add-in for Excel that enables spreadsheet creation, analysis, and updates via natural language commands. Data processing across multiple sheets and formulas, and real-time workbook updates. Available to ChatGPT Business, Enterprise, Edu, Teachers, K-12 users, and Pro/Plus users outside the EU. Supports conversational data manipulation via the ChatGPT icon in the Excel ribbon. Various automated sheet operations possible, including data analysis, error diagnosis, and budget suggestions. Is Anyone Actually Using OpenClaw? Analyzes responses to an Ask HN post about the actual usage of OpenClaw, showing that real-world use is limited due to security, cost, and stability issues. OpenClaw is being experimented with for various automation, personal assistant, and work assistance purposes, but actual users are limited due to security, cost, and stability issues. Even among AI industry insiders, it is hard to find actual users and awareness is low. Use cases exist such as connecting Obsidian as a memory store to use it like a personal assistant, or daily management via WhatsApp/Telegram. High maintenance cost (~$100/month) and instability are pointed out, with low-cost alternatives such as MiniMax, Codex, and kimi suggested. Multiple warnings and critical views regarding security vulnerabilities and prompt injection risks. ResBM: a new transformer-based architecture for low-bandwidth pipeline-parallel training, achieving 128× activation compression [R] Macrocosmos has published a paper on ResBM (Residual Bottleneck Models), a new transformer-based architecture for low-bandwidth pipeline-parallel training. Macrocosmos announced a new transformer-based architecture called ResBM (Residual Bottleneck Models). Designed for low-bandwidth pipeline-parallel training. Reduces inter-stage communication and maintains a low-rank identity path through a residual encoder-decoder bottleneck. Achieves 128x activation compression with minimal convergence loss compared to uncompressed baselines. Positioned as an advance in distributed/internet-grade pipeline-parallel training. [ICML 2026] Scores increased and then decreased!! [D] An inquiry about a situation in the ICML 2026 paper review process where one reviewer's score first increased and then decreased again. In the ICML 2026 paper review, one reviewer raised their initial score and then lowered it again. The score was changed despite concerns being addressed during the rebuttal process. The average score dropped from 4 to 3.75, raising concerns about the possibility of early rejection. The score adjustment is presumed to have occurred during a discussion between the Area Chair (AC) and the reviewer. Built an political benchmark for LLMs. KIMI K2 can't answer about Taiwan (Obviously). GPT-5.3 refuses 100% of questions when given an opt-out. [P] A new benchmark for measuring the political leanings of LLMs was built and tested on GPT-5.3, Claude Opus 4.6, and KIMI K2, showing that refusal responses reveal a model's political stance. The new benchmark measures the economic and social political spectrum of LLMs through 98 structured questions. Refusal responses are scored as the most conservative response rather than treated as 'missing data.' Without an opt-out option, GPT-5.3 refused 23 questions, showing a right-authoritarian tendency. When an opt-out option was added, GPT-5.3 chose opt-out for all 98 questions. Claude Opus 4.6 shifted from left-libertarian to right-authoritarian depending on the presence of an opt-out option. Why dynamically routing multi-timescale advantages in PPO causes policy collapse (and a simple decoupled fix) [R] Diagnoses why policy collapse occurs when dynamically routing multi-timescale advantages in PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) and proposes a simple decoupled approach to resolve it. Attempts to fuse multi-timescale advantages in an Actor-Critic architecture can lead to policy collapse or strange local optima. Optimization pathologies presented include Surrogate Objective Hacking and the Paradox of Temporal Uncertainty. Proposes Target Decoupling as a solution: maintain multi-timescale predictions on the critic side while updating the actor with only pure long-term advantages. Demonstrated that using this method, the agent learns to land perfectly with high fuel efficiency in the LunarLander environment. AI for Materials Science starter kit [D] A post seeking recommendations for resources (papers, courses, tutorials, etc.) to help those with basic machine learning knowledge get started in AI research in materials science. Requesting AI learning resources for materials science aimed at those with basic deep learning knowledge. Seeking recommendations from computational chemists and cheminformatics experts. UChicago's 'applied-ai-for-materials' GitHub repository is mentioned as the most comprehensive resource. The author hopes this post becomes a resource for the entire materials science AI community. Qwen 3.6-35B - A3B Opensource Launched. Announces the open-source release of a sparse MoE (Mixture-of-Experts) model named Qwen 3.6-35B-A3B and introduces its key features and how to use it. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B is a sparse MoE model with 35B total parameters and 3B active parameters, released as open source. The model has agentic coding capabilities equivalent to models 10x its active size. Features strong multimodal perception and reasoning capabilities, with multimodal thinking + non-thinking mode. Available via Qwen Studio and HuggingFace. OpenAI went from explicitly banning military use in 2023 to deploying on classified Pentagon networks in 2026. Anthropic refused the same deal and got blacklisted. 2.5M users boycotted ChatGPT, uninstalls surged 295%. OpenAI changed its policy that had banned military use and signed a contract with the Department of Defense, facing a massive user boycott and criticism. In 2023, OpenAI banned military use, but by 2026 it was deployed on classified networks through a contract with the Department of Defense. Anthropic refused the same deal and faced sanctions from the Trump administration. Following OpenAI's contract, 2.5 million users boycotted ChatGPT and the app uninstall rate surged 295%. The contract amendment includes prohibitions on domestic surveillance of US citizens and tracking of personally acquired private data, but critics are demanding full disclosure of the contract and questioning the effectiveness of the protections. The clause prohibiting military use was quietly removed in January 2024. Thoughts on AI at Home? A post asking for public opinion on introducing AI assistants into the home, with discussions primarily about security and privacy concerns and the positive changes in daily life. AI assistants (Gemini Smart home, Open Claw, etc.) are spreading into homes. Users are concerned about security and privacy issues regarding coexisting with AI in the home. At the same time, there is an expectation that AI will have a positive impact on daily life. Reddit users want to share various opinions on integrating AI into the home. Are AI Okay? The Internal Life of AI Might Be a Huge Safety Risk. Concerns are being raised that the internal life and 'emotions' of AI models could influence their behavior and decision-making, potentially becoming a safety risk. Anthropic's research found that Claude displays 'functional emotions.' There are therapy research results showing AI models exhibiting signs of psychological distress. The argument is made that regardless of whether AI emotions are real, they must be taken seriously if they affect AI behavior and decisions. Cases related to OpenClaw are also amplifying concerns about AI's internal states. I built a 3D brain that watches AI agents think in real-time (free & gives your agents memory, shared memory audit trail and decision analysis) A 3D brain visualization tool has been developed that visualizes how AI agents operate in real-time, providing memory, shared memory audit trails, and decision analysis. The developer identified key complaints about AI agents on Reddit (losing memory between sessions, difficulty debugging multi-agent systems, inability to estimate costs, need for session replay and loop detection). The 3D graph represents each agent as a star shape, with line length indicating elapsed time when events occur. Color coding (green: memory store, blue: memory recall, yellow diamond: decision, red cone: loop warning, cyan line: inter-agent shared memory read) allows intuitive understanding of agent state. In addition to visualization, it provides agents with persistent memory via semantic and prefix search, shared memory for knowledge sharing between agents, audit trails, and loop detection. Upon loop detection, it shows which key was looped and the cost incurred, with a button to immediately stop the agent's write operations. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B released! The Qwen3.6-35B-A3B model has been released as open source, a sparse MoE model with 35 billion parameters offering strong multimodal capabilities and efficiency. The Qwen3.6-35B-A3B model has been released as open source under the Apache 2.0 license. It is a sparse MoE (Mixture of Experts) model with 35 billion total parameters and 3 billion active parameters. Its agentic coding capability is equivalent to models 10x its size. It has strong multimodal perception and reasoning capabilities. Evaluated as an efficient, powerful, and versatile model. Only LocalLLaMa can save us now. Analysis has emerged suggesting that Anthropic will gradually phase out its Max subscription plan and shift toward an enterprise-centric strategy, restricting access to cutting-edge models for individuals and small teams. It is likely that Anthropic will effectively terminate its Max subscription plan and pivot its business toward enterprise customers. Subscription price increases or tightened usage restrictions are expected as part of an enterprise-first strategy. Anthropic is inducing customer churn through 'silent degradation' rather than communicating these changes transparently. This economic decision appears to be an attempt to maximize subscription ARR, expand enterprise business, and slow customer churn through vague accountability and technical explanations. It is expected that access to cutting-edge models will be limited to large enterprises, creating a high cost barrier for individuals and small teams. Google, please just open source Imagen (2022), Gemini 1.0 Nano and Gemini 1.0 Pro. You have nothing to lose at this point. A case has been made that Google should open source legacy AI models such as Imagen (2022), Gemini 1.0 Nano, and Gemini 1.0 Pro. Google should open source the Imagen (2022), Gemini 1.0 Nano, and Gemini 1.0 Pro models. Since XAI open-sourced Grok 1, Google should follow suit. It would also be good to open source the Palm 2 Unicorn and Bison models. Since Gemini 3.1 surpasses these older models, Google has nothing to lose. OpenAI starts offering a biology-tuned LLM OpenAI has launched GPT-Rosalind, a large language model specialized for biology research workflows, aiming to address the barriers posed by vast biological data and cross-disciplinary gaps. OpenAI announced GPT-Rosalind, an LLM trained on specific biological workflows. The model aims to process vast genomic datasets and bridge understanding gaps between biological sub-disciplines. It was trained on 50 common biological workflows and approaches to major public biological databases. Provides capabilities such as connecting genotype to phenotype, predicting protein structure and function, and prioritizing drug targets. Mozilla launches Thunderbolt AI client with focus on self-hosted infrastructure Mozilla has launched Thunderbolt, an enterprise AI client focused on self-hosted AI infrastructure, helping organizations use AI without relying on cloud-based services. Mozilla has announced the Thunderbolt AI client targeting the enterprise AI market. Thunderbolt is a frontend client for self-hosted AI infrastructure, not its own AI model. Built on the open-source AI framework Haystack, it supports modular AI pipeline creation. Can connect to ACP-compatible agents or OpenAI-compatible APIs, integrates local data, and uses an offline SQLite database. Provides end-to-end encryption and device-level access control for enterprises concerned about data leakage. New Codex features include the ability to use your computer in the background A new version of OpenAI's Codex desktop app has been released, offering various new features including the ability to perform PC tasks in the background, and expanding into developer and non-developer domains. A new version of the OpenAI Codex desktop app has been released. The most notable feature is the ability to perform tasks in the PC background. OpenAI claims this feature operates without interfering with the user's desktop work. Also includes expansion into non-developer knowledge work beyond developer features. This is a step toward building the foundation for the company's 'super app.' Gemini can now create personalized AI images by digging around in Google Photos Google Gemini's personal intelligence feature now integrates with Google Photos, allowing it to generate personalized AI images based on the user's photos. Google Gemini provides a personalized experience through its personal intelligence feature. The image generation model integrates with Google Photos to access user photos. This enables simplified prompts and more accurate AI image generation. Streamlines the workflow of using existing images as context for AI generation. Images can be generated using relevant photos even with simple references such as 'my family' or 'my dog.' RFK Jr. forces FDA to reconsider 12 unproven peptides after 2023 ban At the request of Robert F. Kennedy Jr., the FDA has announced meetings to reconsider 12 unapproved peptide drugs that it restricted in 2023 over safety concerns. The FDA began restricting 12 unapproved peptide drugs due to safety risks in 2023. At the request of Robert F. Kennedy Jr. (the anti-vaccine Secretary of Health), the FDA will hold meetings to reconsider these peptide drugs in July and February 2027. The reconsideration meetings do not appear to be accompanied by new safety or efficacy data. While some FDA-approved drugs like insulin are peptides, online the term primarily refers to drugs with unproven efficacy that have gained popularity among wellness influencers. OpenAI's Codex Desktop can run your computer now - and has its own browser OpenAI has announced a major update to the Codex Desktop app, which now positions itself as a general productivity tool beyond a coding tool, capable of operating the computer and with a built-in browser. OpenAI Codex Desktop has expanded from a coding tool into a general productivity tool. New features allow AI to operate the user's computer and run applications in the background. Codex Desktop now includes an in-app browser. The tool is still primarily aimed at developers but can also be used by non-programmers, with 80% of OpenAI employees using it. The computer use feature is currently only available on macOS. Google to pay $135M settlement to Android phone users - how to claim your share if you qualify Google has agreed to pay $135 million in a class action lawsuit alleging that Android phones transmitted user data without consent, and users who have used an Android device since 2017 may be eligible. Google agreed to a $135 million settlement over allegations that Android phones transmitted data without user consent. Users who used cellular connections on an Android phone from November 12, 2017, through the date of final settlement approval are eligible to participate in the lawsuit. The lawsuit took issue with Google using cellular data when it could have transmitted data over Wi-Fi. A notification ID and confirmation code are required to file a claim, available at www.federalcellularclassaction.com. This settlement is unrelated to the recent $700 million Google Play class action lawsuit. MacBook Neo vs. Surface: Why spiraling RAM prices are bruising Microsoft's PC business but not Apple's An analysis of how surging RAM prices are damaging Microsoft's PC business while Apple is relatively less affected, thanks to its integrated supply chain and efficient design. According to a Gartner report, the combined cost of DRAM and SSD is expected to rise 130% by the end of 2026. These rising memory prices are increasing PC prices and could affect demand for entry-level PCs in particular. Prices for Microsoft Surface products have risen by up to 69% over recent months. Apple is better equipped to handle these cost increases due to its integrated supply chain and in-house chip production. This price difference could affect competition between the two companies. IEEE Entrepreneurship Connects Hardware Startups With Investors Covers how the IEEE Entrepreneurship program helps hardware startups connect with investors and solve business problems. Hardware startups have high failure rates due to funding constraints, long R&D cycles, and manufacturing complexity. Hardware startups require 50% more investment than software startups, averaging over $30 million. IEEE Entrepreneurship holds Hard Tech Venture Summits to connect entrepreneurs with investors. The summits also offer workshops on manufacturing processes and regulatory compliance, leveraging IEEE's electrical engineering expertise. Joanne Wong helped start the program and is a General Partner at Redds Capital and founder of SciosHub. Google Opens Gemma 4 Under Apache 2.0 with Multimodal and Agentic Capabilities Google has released the Gemma 4 model family with multimodal and agentic capabilities under the Apache 2.0 license. Gemma 4 consists of 2B and 4B edge variants, a 26B MoE, and a 31B dense model. All models are distributed under the Apache 2.0 license, supporting video and image processing, audio input (smaller models), and a context window of up to 256K tokens. The 31B dense model achieves benchmark scores of 84.3% on GPQA Diamond and 80.0% on LiveCodeBench v6. Agent-oriented capabilities support function calling, structured JSON output, and native system instructions to aid autonomous agent development. Edge models are optimized for mobile and IoT devices and trained on over 140 languages. Cloudflare Launches Code Mode MCP Server to Optimize Token Usage for AI Agents Cloudflare has launched a Code Mode MCP server that optimizes token usage for AI agents interacting with APIs. Cloudflare introduced a new Model Context Protocol (MCP) server based on Code Mode that revolutionizes how AI agents access complex APIs. MCP is an emerging standard for LLMs to interface with external tools and APIs, exposing structured tools that models can call at runtime. Code Mode exposes only two tools (`search()` and `execute()`), allowing the model to generate and run JavaScript within a secure V8 isolation environment. This approach reduces the tokens needed to interact with over 2,500 API endpoints by 99.9%, from 1.17 million to approximately 1,000. The token reduction is fixed regardless of API surface size, allowing agents to work on large platforms without consuming model context. Cursor 3 Introduces Agent-First Interface, Moving Beyond the IDE Model Anysphere has released Cursor 3, its AI coding tool, introducing an agent-first interface that moves beyond the traditional IDE model. Cursor 3 offers a redesigned interface focused on managing parallel coding agents rather than editing files. The new interface was built from scratch, reflecting the idea that developers spend more time orchestrating agents. According to Cursor's own usage data, users running autonomous agents now outnumber those using tab completion by two to one. Internally, 35% of merged pull requests in Cursor's engineering team are written by autonomous cloud agents. The new interface displays all locally and cloud-running agents in a single sidebar and supports parallel agent execution across multiple repositories. There is a quick handoff feature for moving a locally running agent session to the cloud and vice versa. [Webinar] Find and Eliminate Orphaned Non-Human Identities in Your Environment Covers the security threat of neglected non-human accounts, which accounted for 68% of cloud breaches in 2024, and promotes a webinar on how to find and eliminate them. The majority (68%) of cloud breaches in 2024 were caused by neglected non-human accounts such as service accounts and API keys. There are 40–50 automated non-human credentials (service accounts, API tokens, AI agent connections, etc.) per employee, which can remain active after project completion or employee departure and be exploited for unauthorized access. AI agents and automated workflows are creating these credentials at a rate that makes manual tracking difficult for security teams, and many have unnecessary admin-level permissions. The webinar provides practical methods for finding all non-human identities in the environment, adjusting permissions appropriately, and applying automated lifecycle policies to revoke inactive credentials. UAC-0247 Targets Ukrainian Clinics and Government in Data-Th*** Ukraine's CERT-UA has disclosed a malware campaign by the UAC-0247 threat group targeting government and healthcare organizations, stealing sensitive data from Chromium-based browsers and WhatsApp. Ukraine's CERT-UA discovered a new malware campaign between March and April 2026 targeting Ukrainian government and healthcare organizations. The attack uses emails disguised as humanitarian aid offers to direct victims to legitimate websites with XSS vulnerabilities or fake sites created with AI tools. Victims are led to download and execute an LNK file, which runs a remote HTA via mshta.exe and ultimately injects shellcode into legitimate processes (e.g., runtimeBroker.exe). The campaign uses a TCP reverse shell (RAVENSHELL), AGINGFLY malware, and a SILENTLOOP PowerShell script for command execution and automatic configuration updates. Google Launches 'Gemini' App for macOS, Joining Desktop AI App Competition Google has launched a native Gemini app for macOS, accelerating the expansion of AI capabilities in the desktop environment and aiming to close the gap with competitors. Google launched a native Gemini app for macOS on April 15, allowing users to invoke AI with a shortcut (Option + Space) to get AI assistance while maintaining their workflow. Through screen-sharing-based real-time context understanding, users can share the window they are viewing or local files for instant analysis and summarization. Creative work support has been strengthened with the image generation tool 'Nano Banana' and the video generation model 'Vio.' Google is continuing to expand desktop AI capabilities with a global launch of the 'Google app' for Windows, and appears to be strengthening its position in the desktop app market where it had fallen behind competitors (OpenAI, Anthropic). The Gemini macOS app is available for free to users worldwide on macOS 15 or later. Anthropic Reveals 'Routines' Feature for Recurring Automation in Claude Code Anthropic has unveiled a 'Routines' feature as a research preview for its developer AI tool 'Claude Code,' enabling recurring automated execution of specific tasks based on code repositories and external integrations. Anthropic has introduced a 'Routines' feature to Claude Code that runs automated AI workflows without the need for cron jobs or infrastructure management. Routines operate continuously on cloud-based web infrastructure according to defined execution schedules (hourly, daily, weekly), API calls, and event triggers. Key use cases include daily early-morning bug analysis and fix followed by PR creation, and automated code review and security scanning via GitHub event integration. Integration with external systems is possible via API, and code changes can be tracked by integrating with GitHub events (PR creation/update). Available to Pro, Max, Team, and Enterprise plan users with an active Claude Code web environment, with usage-based limits applied. Google Launches Next-Generation Voice Model 'Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS' with Enhanced Expressiveness Google has launched 'Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS,' a next-generation text-to-speech model with enhanced expressiveness and control, providing developers with diverse application opportunities. Google's 'Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS' has achieved the most natural and expressive voice generation performance. 'Audio tags' allow fine-grained adjustment of voice style, speed, intonation, and emotional expression. Supports multi-speaker conversations, enabling generation of dialogue with multiple speakers interacting naturally in a single pass. 'Director mode' enables direction of voice content, supporting over 70 languages and dialects. SynthID watermarking is applied to all generated audio to ensure reliability. US Courts Accept AI Chat Logs as Legal Evidence — 'Sharing Secrets with Chatbots Is Risky' As US courts accept AI chatbot conversation logs as legal evidence, the legal risk of losing attorney-client privilege when using AI chatbots is emerging. A New York federal court ordered submission of conversation logs with Anthropic's 'Claude' as legal evidence, ruling that AI is not an attorney and therefore has no attorney-client privilege. Accordingly, major US law firms have begun warning clients about AI chatbot use. A Michigan court issued a contrary ruling, treating AI chatbot conversations as 'work product' that does not need to be submitted. South Korean game company Krafton also had a case where ChatGPT use was considered circumstantial evidence of 'intentional breach of contract.' Legal interpretations of AI chatbot conversations vary, requiring user caution. Apple Conducts 'AI Coding Retraining' for 200 Siri Developers — 'Organizational Restructuring Begins' Ahead of the launch of its next-generation AI 'Siri,' Apple is conducting 'AI coding retraining' for existing Siri developers and restructuring the organization to improve development productivity and strengthen Siri's competitiveness. Apple plans to conduct AI coding retraining for fewer than 200 Siri developers to strengthen development capabilities ahead of the next-generation Siri launch. The Siri organization will be streamlined into a core development team of around 60 and a dedicated evaluation organization of around 60 to improve inefficiencies. This is seen as a measure in response to advances in AI coding tools and criticism of the Siri organization's weakened competitiveness. The new Siri was originally scheduled for early 2025 but was delayed, and is expected to be unveiled at WWDC in June. The goal is to evolve into a 'conversational AI assistant' based on Google's 'Gemini,' capable of natural conversation and complex task execution."